In most hydraulic systems, components are divided into two main sections:
The actuator and control valves
are mounted on the machine structure
The power unit,
which supplies hydraulic energy to the system
The
hydraulic power unit
consists of several key components, including:
A
pump
to generate fluid flow.
A
reservoir
to store hydraulic fluid.
A
prime mover
, typically an
electric motor
, to drive the pump.
In addition to these primary components, most hydraulic power units include additional elements for monitoring and safety, such as:
Pressure gauges
to track system pressure.
Filters
to remove contaminants from the oil.
Relief valves
to prevent overpressure conditions.
Cooling mechanisms
to maintain optimal fluid temperature.
How a Hydraulic Power Unit Works
When the
prime mover
(electric motor) starts, it rotates the pump shaft. This movement causes the pump to draw oil from the
reservoir
through a
suction filter
and push it into the hydraulic system via the
supply line
.
Once the oil has completed its work within the actuators, it returns to the
reservoir
through the
return line
. This process ensures a continuous flow of hydraulic fluid to power the system.
Beyond supplying fluid, the power unit must also ensure the
oil remains clean and within a safe temperature range
. To accomplish this, most power units incorporate:
Filtration systems
, including suction and return filters, to remove contaminants.
Cooling systems
, such as heat exchangers using air or water, to dissipate excess heat. Some units rely on
large reservoirs
to allow oil to cool naturally before recirculating.
Key Components of a Hydraulic Power Unit
Figure \(\PageIndex{2}\): Hydraulic power unit
Electric Motor
Most industrial hydraulic systems use a
constant-speed electric motor
to drive the pump. The motor's shaft is connected to the pump shaft through a
coupling
, allowing efficient transfer of mechanical power.
Hydraulic Pump
The
hydraulic pump
generates fluid flow. As the electric motor drives the pump, it, in a way, pulls oil from the
reservoir
through its
inlet
and forces it into the system via its
outlet
.
Reservoir
The
reservoir
serves multiple functions:
It
stores hydraulic fluid
when not in use.
It allows
dirt and contaminants
to settle
before re-entering the system.
It helps
dissipate heat
, keeping the fluid at an appropriate temperature.
Suction Line
The
suction line
connects the
pump inlet
to the reservoir, allowing the pump to draw in oil.
Supply Line (Pressure Line)
The
supply line
connects to the
pump outlet
and delivers pressurized oil to the hydraulic system. This line runs through the
relief valve manifold block
and exits through a port usually labeled
“P”
.
Sometimes, a supply line will distribute oil to a
supply manifold
, a machined block with multiple ports that provide hydraulic fluid to various system components.
Return Line
The
return line
is responsible for directing oil from the hydraulic system back to the reservoir. This ensures continuous circulation of hydraulic fluid, allowing it to be reused in the system.
Relief Valve
The
relief valve
is a crucial safety component that prevents excessive pressure from building up in the system. It is designed to open when system pressure exceeds a preset limit, allowing excess fluid to return to the reservoir and maintain safe operating conditions. The pressure setting can be adjusted using a control knob located on the valve body.
Suction Strainer
Located within the
suction line
at the pump inlet and submerged in hydraulic fluid, the
suction strainer
plays a critical role in protecting the pump from contaminants. It acts as a first line of defense, preventing large dirt particles from entering the pump and causing damage.
Filler/Breather
The
filler/breather
serves two essential functions in a hydraulic power unit:
Reservoir Filling
– The unit features a removable cap that allows hydraulic oil to be added to the reservoir. A protective screen is often included to prevent large contaminants from entering during filling.
Airflow Regulation
– Since the fluid level fluctuates during system operation, the
filler/breather
ensures that air can enter or exit the reservoir, maintaining a pressure balance close to atmospheric levels. Air passes through filtered openings in the breather cap to prevent dust and debris from contaminating the fluid.
Liquid Level Gauge
Often, there is a
liquid level gauge
that provides a visual indication of the amount of oil in the reservoir. There are two common types:
Oil level gauge
– This gauge is mounted externally on the reservoir and is connected to the oil inside, providing an accurate reading.
Sight glass
– A small, circular glass window mounted at the correct oil level on the reservoir, allowing operators to verify the fluid level at a glance.
Return Filter
Sometimes, a
return filter
is installed in the return line to remove contaminants that may have been picked up as the oil circulated through the system. By filtering the returning fluid before it re-enters the reservoir, it helps maintain hydraulic fluid cleanliness and prolongs the lifespan of system components.